// `std:args` — the program's command-line arguments, and the last piece of
// VL's I/O tier.
//
// Until `std:fs` landed a VL program could compute and print, nothing else.
// This is the other half of the same sentence: a program that can read a file
// but cannot be told WHICH file is still a program with its inputs compiled in.
// WHAT ADMITS THIS MODULE is `std-design.md`'s inventory clause — "what the
// LANGUAGE story needs to be complete without third parties … eventually
// fs/io/args once WASI lands", which names `std:args` outright among the first
// WASI-era additions. It is NOT admitted by "a consumer in the tree", and the
// distinction matters because that consumer does not exist yet: `fuzz-vl.sh:82`
// still sed-rewrites `fuzzgen.vl`'s source
// (`s/^let RICHVALUES = .*/let RICHVALUES = $VALUES/`), so renaming a `let`
// still silently disables a flag. ROADMAP ranks `argv` value #1 of the host-ABI
// dogfooding item for exactly that reason, and converting `fuzzgen.vl` is the
// FOLLOW-UP rather than the warrant — it is also not free, because the host wart
// below makes a `--values` flag unspellable without `--`.
//
// ONE EXPORT, and that is the whole surface. `std-design.md` D1's admission
// principle says nothing lands without a consumer in the tree, and the consumer
// above needs exactly one thing: the positional arguments, as strings. Every
// other shape this module could have had is named at the bottom together with
// the condition that would admit it. Adding a name later is purely additive;
// std has no deprecation story, so the name that does not exist yet is the only
// one still free to be right.
//
// INDEX 0 IS THE FIRST USER ARGUMENT. There is no program name in this list —
// not at index 0, not anywhere, and no export returns one. That is the FLOOR's
// shape, not a re-indexing this module applied: `vl run p.vl a b` gives
// `__args_count__() == 2` with `a` at index 0 (measured). Every language a VL
// programmer arrives from disagrees — C's `argv[0]`, `sys.argv[0]`,
// `os.Args[0]` and `process.argv[0..1]` are all the program — so this is the
// one fact about the module worth reading twice, and `x.slice(1)` is the habit
// to unlearn rather than a step to keep.
//
// The host is right to omit it. Under `vl run p.vl a b` there is no honest
// answer: `vl` is the process, `p.vl` is the source, and a program that printed
// either as its own name would print usage text naming the wrong thing. A
// compiled VL binary will have a real argv[0] one day and it should arrive as
// its own export (`programName(): string | null`) rather than as a slot that
// means different things on different hosts.
//
// NAMED `programArgs` AND NOT `args`, and this one is measured rather than
// preferred. VL has no namespace import, so every export lands in the
// importer's flat top-level scope — and a module that both imports and declares
// a name is a HARD PARSE ERROR, not a shadowing warning:
//
//     Duplicate binding "args": module `m.vl` both imports and declares it
//
// So an export called `args` makes `const args = args()` — the first line every
// consumer of this module wants to write — refuse to compile, and the fix
// (`as` on the import) is a tax on every caller forever. `programArgs` is the
// same reasoning `std:fs` used for `pathExists` over `exists`, applied to a
// name whose collision is not hypothetical but the call site itself.
// `argv` was the other candidate and is worse than either: it is the one
// spelling that actively promises the C layout this floor does not have.
//
// NOT `self`-FIRST, so this is a free function rather than a UFCS method. `self`
// is the UFCS switch (`std:fs`'s header explains it), and here it is forced
// rather than chosen: argv has no receiver. There is nothing to be a method ON.
//
// A LIST, NOT `argCount()` + `argAt(i)`. The floor is a count plus an indexed
// getter and this module deliberately does not re-export that shape:
//
//   - `string[]` reaches the generic surface and `u8[]` does not. `indexOf`,
//     `includes`, `mapIndexed` and `reduce` from `std:array`, and `join` from
//     `std:fmt`, all apply to the result of this call. An index protocol
//     composes with nothing.
//   - It dissolves the out-of-range question instead of answering it. `T | null`
//     for a missing argument would put a check on every read; a trap would kill
//     a program because its USER forgot an argument, which is not a bug in the
//     program and so is not what traps are for
//     (`docs/error-handling-design.md`). With a list there is no third policy to
//     invent: `.length` is the count, `[i]` is VL's ordinary bounds-checked
//     index, and indexing past a length you were handed is a bug exactly like
//     any other out-of-bounds index.
//   - `listDir(path): string[] | IoError` is the closest structural sibling in
//     std — a host holding a list of byte strings, handed over as names — and it
//     materialises the list rather than exposing a cursor. Two modules over one
//     floor should not disagree about that.
//
// FALLIBLE, AND THE FAILURE ARM IS CURRENTLY UNREACHABLE. `__args_get__`
// returns `u8[]`, so the floor's contract is BYTES, and bytes are not
// necessarily UTF-8: a POSIX argument is any NUL-free byte string. But no host
// in this tree can deliver one — both Rust hosts read `std::env::args()`, which
// PANICS on a non-Unicode argument, so `vl run p.vl $'\xff'` aborts the process
// with exit 101 before VL is instantiated (measured), and the V8 harness cannot
// call the intrinsic at all. The error arm is dead code today, and it ships anyway
// because the two mistakes are not the same size: a total `string[]` that meets
// a WASI-era host reading `args_get` must either corrupt silently or crash, and
// widening a return type afterwards is the breaking change std has no
// deprecation story for. One dead `is` branch is the cheap wrong answer.
//
// THE FAILURE IS `Utf8Error`, BORROWED FROM `std:utf8` RATHER THAN INVENTED.
// This module has exactly ONE failure domain — an argument that is not text —
// and `std:utf8` already owns both the decoder and the name for it, carrying
// the offset and the offending byte. Two alternatives were rejected:
//
//   - A new `ArgError = { code: i32, msg: string }` is STRUCTURALLY IDENTICAL to
//     `std:fs`'s `IoError` — VL aliases are structural — so a program spelling
//     `T | IoError | ArgError` would name one type twice. `std:utf8`'s header
//     records the same trap being dodged for `ParseError`; walking into it here
//     would be worse, because the collision is with a type that already exists.
//   - Importing `IoError` FROM `std:fs` costs five host imports this module
//     never calls. Measured at this head: a module importing so much as a type
//     from `std:fs` declares `__fs_read__`, `__fs_write__`, `__fs_list__`,
//     `__fs_stat__` and `__fs_errno__`, because there is no cross-module dead-
//     code elimination. As written this module declares three
//     (`__args_count__`, `__args_get__`, `__fs_errno__`) and every one of them
//     is called. A host that wants to run an argv program — the playground's
//     runtime supplies seven print imports and nothing else — should not have to
//     fake a filesystem to do it.
//
// `std:fs` FOLDS its decode failure into `IoError` (EILSEQ) and this module does
// not, which is a difference in the number of failure domains and not in taste:
// `readTextFile` can fail as a READ or as a DECODE and one error type spares its
// caller a two-arm union, while everything here that can go wrong is a decode.
// Folding would only throw `at` and `byte` away.
//
// ALL OR NOTHING. One argument that is not UTF-8 fails the WHOLE call. Dropping
// it would RENUMBER every argument after it — the second positional silently
// becomes the first — and in a list whose positions are its meaning that turns a
// bad input into a wrong answer instead of a loud one. `listDir` refuses for the
// same reason and the reason is stronger here.
//
// THE ERRNO STAYS INSIDE. `std:fs` exposes `__fs_errno__`'s ambient cell in its
// contract because its callers branch on the code; here no caller can, so the
// empty-plus-errno protocol is read once inside the loop and never surfaces.
// This module has no ambient state in its API and no order-dependent call.
//
// ONE `__trap__`, AND IT IS NOT A FAILURE MODE. `std:fs` opens by saying its
// count is zero; this module's is one, so it is named here rather than found.
// It fires only if the floor refuses an index BELOW the count it just reported —
// a host contradicting itself, which is a bug and not a condition any caller can
// cause or handle. Nothing a user can type reaches it.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO.
//
// NO FLAG PARSING — not "not yet here", but a different module. Every one of
// short vs long, clustered `-abc`, `--name=value` vs `--name value`, `--` as
// end-of-options, a repeated flag (last wins, or accumulate), `-1` as a value
// rather than a flag, and typed/required/defaulted values is a policy decision,
// and a parser that decides half of them silently disagrees with whatever the
// caller assumed about the other half. A half-parser is worse than none. It also
// wants a map to return its results into and `std:map` does not exist yet. None
// of it needs a new floor intrinsic — a parser is a pure `string[] => …` over
// what this returns — so deferring costs nothing and buys the whole design.
//
// NO BYTES ACCESSOR. `std:fs` offers `readFile` beside `readTextFile` because a
// PNG must survive the round trip; the argv equivalent would be a non-UTF-8 path
// argument, and there is nowhere to put one: every `std:fs` entry point takes
// `path: string`, so the bytes could not be used to open the file they name.
// When `std:fs` grows a bytes-taking path — or when process spawn arrives — a
// `programArgBytes(i): u8[]` becomes admissible; today it has no consumer.
//
// NO LOSSY VARIANT. `decodeUtf8Lossy` is `std:utf8`'s deliberate opt-out for a
// caller who would rather render mojibake than stop, and no argv consumer in
// this tree wants it: a mangled argument is a wrong filename or a wrong number,
// not a log line to squint at. It becomes admissible on the same day the bytes
// accessor does, and out of the same export.
//
// NO ENVIRONMENT, NO EXIT CODE, NO PROGRAM NAME, NO STDIN. Each is a separate
// floor intrinsic that does not exist (`env` + `exit` are item 5 of the same
// ROADMAP list and a process exit code sits inside item 4), and a std wrapper
// for a syscall VL cannot make is a name that fails at emit.
//
// ONE HOST WART, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS INVISIBLE FROM IN HERE. `vl run p.vl -v x`
// delivers ONE argument, `x`: the host's own flag loop matches `--compiler`,
// `-e` and `--`, routes every other non-dash token into the vector, and falls
// through anything else beginning with `-` to a catch-all that discards it with
// no diagnostic (measured). `vl run p.vl -- -v x` delivers both. Nothing in this
// module can tell the two apart — a dropped argument and an un-passed one are
// the same absence — so a VL program taking dash-led arguments must be invoked
// after `--`, and `tests/vl_std_args_test.ts` pins the behaviour so a change to
// it is noticed rather than silently reshaping what programs see. Filed as
// issue #1818; a fix there makes this paragraph stale rather than wrong, and the
// pinning test is what will say so.

import { Utf8Error, decodeUtf8 } from "std:utf8"

// RE-EXPORTED so a caller of the fallible export can spell its own error arm from
// the module it imported: `import { Utf8Error, programArgs } from "std:args"`.
// Without this the caller needs a second import from `std:utf8` to write the `is`
// branch, which is a tax this module otherwise works hard to avoid. It is not a
// second `Utf8Error` — a re-export records a NAME, so there is exactly one type;
// and it costs nothing, measured: the import section stays at three host imports,
// all of them called. `compiler/emit_base.vl` republishes seven type names from
// `typecheck.vl` on the same mechanism.
export { Utf8Error } from "std:utf8"

// `programArgs()` — every argument the program was given, in order, as text.
//
// Index 0 is the FIRST USER ARGUMENT and there is no program name in the list
// (see the header). No arguments is an EMPTY ARRAY, never an error and never
// null: being given nothing is not a failure, it is the answer.
//
// The failure is a single argument that is not UTF-8, reported as `std:utf8`'s
// `Utf8Error` with `at` and `byte` keeping the meanings that module gives them —
// the offset WITHIN the offending argument, and the byte found there. WHICH
// argument is named in `msg` only, deliberately: a fourth field would fork the
// error vocabulary for a fact no caller can act on, since an argument that is
// not text cannot be repaired by knowing its index.
//
//     import { programArgs } from "std:args"
//     import { Utf8Error } from "std:utf8"
//
//     const args = programArgs()
//     if args is Utf8Error { print(args.msg) } else { print(args.length) }
export function programArgs(): string[] | Utf8Error {
  const n = __args_count__()
  const out: string[] = []
  let i = 0
  while i < n {
    const raw = __args_get__(i)
    // EMPTY is the floor's failure signal AND a legitimate argument: `""` is a
    // real thing to pass (`p.vl "" x` reports a count of 2 with a zero-length
    // argument 0 and errno 0 — measured), so the errno is what separates them,
    // exactly as `std:fs` separates a failed read from an empty file.
    //
    // Inside `0 .. __args_count__()` the floor answers by contract, so a
    // non-zero errno here is a HOST that contradicted its own count between two
    // calls. That is the one condition in this module that is a BUG rather than
    // a failure — nothing the caller passed could cause it and there is no
    // recovery — so it traps with a reason, which is the same call `std:utf8`
    // makes for its own provably-unreachable arm. Checking is what stops the
    // slot silently becoming `""`, which is the failure mode of NOT checking:
    // `decodeUtf8` of an empty array is `""`, so an unchecked floor violation
    // would put a blank argument in a real position and renumber nothing.
    //
    // This is the ONLY `__trap__` in the module, and it is unreachable at both
    // Rust hosts because `__args_get__` ZEROES THE SHARED ERRNO CELL on every
    // success. That cell is `std:fs`'s (`__fs_errno__`) and is per-instance, so
    // this module's one trap rests on a `std:fs` FLOOR INVARIANT rather than on
    // anything local: a floor that stopped zeroing on success would turn a
    // legitimate EMPTY argument into a trap, because empty-plus-errno is exactly
    // how this protocol spells failure. (An earlier version of this comment
    // credited `PROGRAM_ARGS` being filled once and never mutated. That is true
    // and is NOT the guard — the adversarial case is a dirty errno cell left by
    // an unrelated failed `std:fs` call followed by a legitimately empty
    // argument, which `PROGRAM_ARGS` immutability does nothing about.)
    // It needs no `return` behind it — `__trap__` lowers to `unreachable` and
    // the loop is not a return position, so nothing has to be narrowed. That is
    // why `decodeUtf8Lossy`'s `return ""` has no twin here: that one exists to
    // make its `string` return type provable, not to stop execution (verified —
    // this function checks, emits and runs identically with the `return`
    // removed, and a `__trap__` inside a loop halts rather than continuing).
    if raw.length == 0 {
      if __fs_errno__() != 0 {
        __trap__("std:args: the floor refused an index below __args_count__()")
      }
    }
    const s = decodeUtf8(raw)
    if s is Utf8Error {
      // Re-spelled as a literal rather than forwarded, so the message names the
      // argument; a struct produced by a CALL and flowing into a union position
      // does not lower at this head (`std:fs`'s header records the same
      // constraint), so every error out of this module is written where it is
      // returned.
      return {
        at: s.at,
        byte: s.byte,
        msg: "args.programArgs: argument " +
          toString(i) +
          " is not UTF-8: " +
          s.msg,
      }
    }
    out.push(s)
    i = i + 1
  }
  out
}
